index 0000000..c6791ee
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/rd_flow_64.md
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# How to Use
+
+RD Flow 64 by Retro Diffusion is a 271M-parameter pixel-space flow transformer that draws 64x64 pixel art. Every
+pixel is an exact palette colour or fully transparent: a palette-pointer head picks one entry of a palette that the
+model extracts while it draws, or of a palette you lock. Output is a 64x64 RGBA PNG; there is no VAE.
+
+The weights are licensed for non-commercial use only (RD Flow 64 Model License); images you make are yours.
+
+## Download weights
+
+- Drawing model: [Retro-Diffusion/rd-flow-64](https://huggingface.co/Retro-Diffusion/rd-flow-64) `rd-flow-64.safetensors`
+- Prompt reader: the same repository's `text_encoder/model.safetensors` (the first 27 layers of Qwen3-VL-4B-Instruct).
+  Its tensors are named without the `model.` prefix, so rename them once, for example:
+
+```python
+from safetensors import safe_open
+from safetensors.torch import save_file
+with safe_open("text_encoder/model.safetensors", "pt") as f:
+    save_file({"model." + k: f.get_tensor(k) for k in f.keys()}, "rd-flow-64-text-encoder.safetensors")
+```
+
+  Then optionally quantize it (Q8_0 is 3.3 GB and draws the same images):
+  `sd-cli -M convert -m rd-flow-64-text-encoder.safetensors --type q8_0 -o rd-flow-64-text-encoder-Q8_0.gguf`
+
+## Examples
+
+```
+sd-cli --diffusion-model rd-flow-64.safetensors --llm rd-flow-64-text-encoder-Q8_0.gguf --backend te=cpu \
+  -p "<a 120-200 word caption>" -W 64 -H 64 --steps 16 --cfg-scale 4 --rng cpu
+```
+
+- `--palette "#1a1c2c,#5d275d,..."` locks every pixel to those colours (sd-server: `"palette"`).
+- `--opaque-background` draws a full-frame picture instead of a transparent sprite (sd-server: `"opaque_background": true`).
+- The model was trained on long, plain captions (120-200 words: the subject part by part, pose, view, framing,
+  background, outline and shading); short prompts work much worse. The prompting guide in
+  [Retro-Diffusion/rd-flow-64](https://github.com/Retro-Diffusion/rd-flow-64) has a system prompt for an LLM to
+  write them.
+- `--rng cpu` reproduces the reference PyTorch pipeline's noise for a seed. Guidance 3-4 works best.
+- The prompt reader is read once per prompt; on the CPU it takes about 1.5 s, so `--backend te=cpu` keeps VRAM use
+  near 1 GB.
